## Proof

**Definitions.** Let $G$ be a group. A *mean* on $G$ is a positive linear functional $m \colon \ell^\infty(G) \to \mathbb{R}$ with $m(\mathbf{1}) = 1$. It is *left-invariant* if $m(L_g f) = m(f)$ for all $g \in G$ and $f \in \ell^\infty(G)$, where $(L_g f)(x) = f(g^{-1}x)$. It is *right-invariant* if $m(R_g f) = m(f)$ for all $g \in G$, where $(R_g f)(x) = f(xg)$.

**Claim.** If $G$ admits a left-invariant mean, then $G$ admits a right-invariant mean.

**Construction.** Let $m$ be a left-invariant mean on $G$. Define the inversion operator $T \colon \ell^\infty(G) \to \ell^\infty(G)$ by
$$(Tf)(x) := f(x^{-1}).$$
This is well-defined since $x \mapsto x^{-1}$ is a bijection of $G$. Now set
$$m'(f) := m(Tf) = m\bigl(f(\,\cdot^{-1}\,)\bigr).$$

**$m'$ is a mean.** Positivity: if $f \geq 0$ pointwise, then $Tf \geq 0$ pointwise, so $m'(f) = m(Tf) \geq 0$. Normalization: $T\mathbf{1} = \mathbf{1}$, so $m'(\mathbf{1}) = m(\mathbf{1}) = 1$. Linearity is immediate from linearity of $T$ and $m$.

**$m'$ is right-invariant.** We compute $T(R_g f)$. For any $x \in G$:
$$(T(R_g f))(x) = (R_g f)(x^{-1}) = f(x^{-1} g).$$
Since $x^{-1}g = (g^{-1}x)^{-1}$, we have
$$f(x^{-1}g) = f\bigl((g^{-1}x)^{-1}\bigr) = (Tf)(g^{-1}x) = (L_g(Tf))(x).$$
Therefore $T(R_g f) = L_g(Tf)$. Using the left-invariance of $m$:
$$m'(R_g f) = m\bigl(T(R_g f)\bigr) = m\bigl(L_g(Tf)\bigr) = m(Tf) = m'(f).$$

This holds for every $g \in G$, so $m'$ is a right-invariant mean. $\blacksquare$

